[#:seon.task{:name "a-scheduled-collection-runs-inside-the-schedule-proc-step-and-reports-as-in-world"
             :kind :function
             :intent "2026-09-29 runtime_status ranked an over-bound warning on `seon.flow/in-world` (19 calls, mean 12.9 s, max 243,287 ms). Verified cause: in-world (flow.clj:260) is a scope wrapper that binds a world and runs its callback, so its inclusive time is whatever the step or io submission does (flow.clj:374,381,503); the schedule proc's step runs `seon.schedule/fire-due!` -> invoke-handler (schedule.clj:683) synchronously, so the 03:00 UTC `seon.maintenance/collect!` was measured as in-world. Receipt d1fa7e155d93 started 2026-09-29T03:00:00.012Z, completed 03:04:03.180Z = 243,168 ms, matching the max within 120 ms; 19 calls x 12.9 s = 245 s is that one call plus 18 trivial ones. The next day's collection (receipt dc556baef2aa) took 03:09:15 to 03:14:37 = 322 s on the same ~1 GB store; on the 85 MB store it takes 28 s (receipt e902705f9f85). Two defects: (1) the finding names the wrong subject, the same class as task seon.profile/scope-wrappers-report-callback-time-as-work (scope wrappers report callback time as work), so fix it there once for every wrapper rather than special-casing in-world; (2) a real cost the finding hid: the collection holds the single schedule proc for minutes, so no other schedule fires meanwhile, and over ten seconds needs an owner-authorized bound and issue (CLAUDE.md), see task seon.maintenance/collection-marks-slowly-and-records-nothing for the sweep-permit cause. Done when a scheduled job's duration is reported against the job's own handler (`seon.maintenance/collect!`) with its declared bound, in-world no longer appears over-bound for work it only wraps, and collection no longer runs on the schedule proc thread (or the proc's other tasks are shown not to wait). Not established: the per-JVM profile of 2026-09-29 is gone, so the in-world cell was not read directly; the match rests on receipt timings."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/in-world seon.maintenance/collect! seon.schedule/fire-due! seon.schedule/invoke-handler}
             :evidence #{"resources/seon/tasks/seon.maintenance.edn" "resources/seon/tasks/seon.profile.edn"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-schedule-test-root-page-members-differ-after-a-firing"
             :kind :namespace
